=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The BIND DNS resolver daemon (`named`) on srv1, specifically the `recursion no` directive in `/etc/bind/named.conf`.

**Mechanism:** srv1 acts as the campus DNS server for all client subnets. Every client host in the lab (pc1/10.1.0.100, pc2/10.1.0.101, pc3/10.3.0.100, laptop1/10.6.0.100) has `/etc/resolv.conf` pointing exclusively to `nameserver 10.5.0.4` (srv1). With `recursion no` set, BIND will only answer queries for its own authoritative zone (`jeremysitlab.com`) and returns `REFUSED` with `recursion not available` for every external name (e.g. `google.com`). Because browsers and applications must resolve a hostname before opening a TCP connection, all web traffic stalls at the DNS step — the connection never begins, so pages "spin and time out." Direct IP-addressed traffic (ICMP, etc.) bypasses DNS and continues to work, confirming the WAN path and NAT on r1 are fully functional.

**Localization:** srv1 (10.5.0.4), file `/etc/bind/named.conf`, `options { recursion no; }`.

**Root cause:** BIND on srv1 has recursion disabled, preventing it from resolving any name outside the `jeremysitlab.com` authoritative zone; since all clients rely on this single resolver, internet-bound hostname lookups fail network-wide.

**Fix:** In `/etc/bind/named.conf`, change `recursion no;` to `recursion yes;` and optionally add `forwarders { 203.0.113.1; };` (ispa) or equivalent upstream resolvers. Then reload BIND with `rndc reload` or `systemctl reload named`.
=== END ===